{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1477 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8cb9c28ea05aea29c949e692a0fa2b797103fc2d68c8e5095e460b34ce737ad1" :edb/db-before-t 1476 :edb/db-after-t 1477 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534789 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:58:04.927Z" 13194139534789 true] [17592186087780 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310304 13194139534789 true] [17592186087780 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310310 13194139534789 true] [17592186088265 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534789 true] [17592186088265 1100 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation\n\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} = 1$?" 13194139534789 false] [17592186088265 1100 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" 13194139534789 true] [17592186088265 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$" 13194139534789 true] [17592186302588 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/c71a52cc148f8ce4a23a2fb2005757c9d141d3caf05586d8fb5122f591dc7474.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534789 false] [17592186302588 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041013/assets/c71a52cc148f8ce4a23a2fb2005757c9d141d3caf05586d8fb5122f591dc7474.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534789 true] [17592186302620 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/be8468d84e3820bd1e9181d31aa3a1b7d1a85c743020a69cb67fc3f601a88363.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534789 false] [17592186302620 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041013/assets/be8468d84e3820bd1e9181d31aa3a1b7d1a85c743020a69cb67fc3f601a88363.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310305 13194139534789 true] [17592186310304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310308 13194139534789 true] [17592186310304 1089 "q-279165" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534789 true] [17592186310304 1100 "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139534789 true] [17592186310304 1113 #uuid "1f865070-f5b3-5500-a742-b83f4ec5d86b" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310304 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=4.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=4,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=3,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2=4.$" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310306 13194139534789 true] [17592186310305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310307 13194139534789 true] [17592186310305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534789 true] [17592186310305 1041 "field-1" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310307 13194139534789 true] [17592186310305 1110 #uuid "de0b8d34-f76b-543f-ad36-2d004571145a" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310306 1034 "3" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534789 true] [17592186310306 1111 #uuid "51a28714-a125-53b7-8a56-9d8e5eb5fd8e" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310307 1034 "6" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534789 true] [17592186310307 1111 #uuid "33472bbc-2958-5b76-9fd7-e4be00b37d70" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310309 13194139534789 true] [17592186310308 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534789 true] [17592186310308 1041 "field-2" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310308 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310309 13194139534789 true] [17592186310308 1110 #uuid "8274bf2f-e625-59e7-8f11-4a513638b6af" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310309 1034 "4" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534789 true] [17592186310309 1111 #uuid "bc2e44ce-3a5b-51df-aea3-bff0213280ed" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310310 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310311 13194139534789 true] [17592186310310 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310313 13194139534789 true] [17592186310310 1089 "q-279166" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310310 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534789 true] [17592186310310 1100 "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139534789 true] [17592186310310 1113 #uuid "a7f34816-fae1-5bab-b494-08ab8f5462c7" 13194139534789 true] [17592186310310 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}